{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Hotkeys supports multi-key sequences in Vue, where keys are pressed one after another rather than simultaneously."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sequence steps use the same string syntax as single hotkeys. For example, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"['[KeyG]', '[KeyG]']"},{"type":"text","value":" follows a physical position, while "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"['G', 'G']"},{"type":"text","value":" follows the logical letter. A sequence can mix forms, such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"['Mod+[KeyK]', 'C']"},{"type":"text","value":". Display steps with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sequence.map((step) => formatForDisplay(step)).join(' → ')"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reactive-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reactive options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sequence options support the same "},{"type":"link","href":"./hotkeys.md#property-getters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"property getter patterns"}]},{"type":"text","value":" as hotkey options, including "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enabled"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"timeout"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"target"},{"type":"text","value":". Updates preserve the registration when its sequence, target, and platform stay the same."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"basic-usage","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic usage"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { useHotkeySequence } from '@tanstack/vue-hotkeys'\n\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => {\n  window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'smooth' })\n})\n</script>","lang":"vue"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"many-sequences-at-once","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Many sequences at once"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you need several sequences, or a reactive list whose length changes, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useHotkeySequences"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of many "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useHotkeySequence"},{"type":"text","value":" calls. One composable registers every sequence safely."}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { useHotkeySequences } from '@tanstack/vue-hotkeys'\n\nuseHotkeySequences([\n  { sequence: ['G', 'G'], callback: () => scrollToTop() },\n  { sequence: ['D', 'D'], callback: () => deleteLine(), options: { timeout: 500 } },\n])\n</script>","lang":"vue"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Options merge like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useHotkeys"},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HotkeysProvider"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults, then "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commonOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", then each definition's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"options"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"matching-steps","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Matching steps"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SequenceManager"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createSequenceMatcher"},{"type":"text","value":" ignore modifier-only events, IME composition, and automatic keydown repeats. These events neither advance the sequence nor refresh its timeout: holding G does not complete a two-press G sequence. The manager prefers exact matches over weaker logical-key fallbacks while preserving equally strong matches."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"sequence-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sequence options"}]},{"type":"code","value":"useHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], callback, {\n  timeout: 1000,\n  enabled: true,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"reactive-enabled","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reactive "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enabled"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When disabled, the sequence stays registered (visible in devtools); only execution is suppressed."}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { ref } from 'vue'\nimport { useHotkeySequence } from '@tanstack/vue-hotkeys'\n\nconst isVimMode = ref(true)\n\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop(), {\n  enabled: isVimMode,\n})\n</script>","lang":"vue"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"global-defaults-via-provider","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global defaults via provider"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { HotkeysProvider } from '@tanstack/vue-hotkeys'\n</script>\n\n<template>\n  <HotkeysProvider\n    :default-options=\"{\n      hotkeySequence: { timeout: 1500 },\n    }\"\n  >\n    <AppContent />\n  </HotkeysProvider>\n</template>","lang":"vue"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"meta","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sequences support the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":" option as hotkeys. Attach a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"description"},{"type":"text","value":" to use in shortcut palettes and devtools."}]},{"type":"code","value":"useHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Go to Top', description: 'Scroll to the top of the page' },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./hotkeys.md#metadata-name--description","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hotkeys Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for details on declaration merging and introspecting registrations."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"chained-modifier-chords","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chained modifier chords"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This example follows physical R and T positions. Brackets retain those positions even when the keys produce different letters. Other sequences can continue using logical characters."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each step can use modifiers (for example "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Mod+K"},{"type":"text","value":" then "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Mod+C"},{"type":"text","value":"). You can use the same modifier on consecutive steps:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"useHotkeySequence(['Shift+[KeyR]', 'Shift+[KeyT]'], () => doNextAction())","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While a sequence is in progress, modifier-only keydown events (Shift, Control, Alt, or Meta pressed alone) are ignored: they do not advance the sequence and do not reset progress. A user can press Shift alone between "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Shift+R"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Shift+T"},{"type":"text","value":" without breaking the sequence."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"common-patterns","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Common patterns"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"vim-style-navigation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vim-style navigation"}]},{"type":"code","value":"useHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop())\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'Shift+G'], () => scrollToBottom())\nuseHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine())\nuseHotkeySequence(['D', 'W'], () => deleteWord())\nuseHotkeySequence(['C', 'I', 'W'], () => changeInnerWord())","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"konami-code","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Konami Code"}]},{"type":"code","value":"useHotkeySequence(\n  ['ArrowUp', 'ArrowUp', 'ArrowDown', 'ArrowDown', 'B', 'A'],\n  () => enableEasterEgg(),\n  { timeout: 2000 },\n)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"under-the-hood","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Under the hood"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useHotkeySequence"},{"type":"text","value":" uses the singleton "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SequenceManager"},{"type":"text","value":". You can also access it directly:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createSequenceMatcher, getSequenceManager } from '@tanstack/vue-hotkeys'\n\nconst manager = getSequenceManager()\nconst matcher = createSequenceMatcher(['G', 'G'], { timeout: 1000 })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"conflicting-registrations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Conflicting registrations"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For the same target, duplicate detection compares resolved steps: modifier aliases, modifier order, and logical key casing do not create separate bindings. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"conflictBehavior"},{"type":"text","value":" applies to equivalent sequences without rewriting their stored strings. Physical and logical identities remain distinct, and a shared prefix alone is not a duplicate registration. Recorder conflict detection also checks prefixes and observed physical/logical overlap."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"reactive-options","text":"Reactive options","level":2},{"id":"basic-usage","text":"Basic usage","level":2},{"id":"many-sequences-at-once","text":"Many sequences at once","level":2},{"id":"matching-steps","text":"Matching steps","level":2},{"id":"sequence-options","text":"Sequence options","level":2},{"id":"reactive-enabled","text":"Reactive enabled","level":3},{"id":"global-defaults-via-provider","text":"Global defaults via provider","level":2},{"id":"meta","text":"meta","level":3},{"id":"chained-modifier-chords","text":"Chained modifier chords","level":2},{"id":"common-patterns","text":"Common patterns","level":2},{"id":"vim-style-navigation","text":"Vim-style navigation","level":3},{"id":"konami-code","text":"Konami Code","level":3},{"id":"under-the-hood","text":"Under the hood","level":2},{"id":"conflicting-registrations","text":"Conflicting registrations","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Hotkeys supports multi-key sequences in Vue, where keys are pressed one after another rather than simultaneously.\n\nSequence steps use the same string syntax as single hotkeys. For example, `['[KeyG]', '[KeyG]']` follows a physical position, while `['G', 'G']` follows the logical letter. A sequence can mix forms, such as `['Mod+[KeyK]', 'C']`. Display steps with `sequence.map((step) => formatForDisplay(step)).join(' → ')`.\n\n## Reactive options\n\nSequence options support the same [property getter patterns](./hotkeys.md#property-getters) as hotkey options, including `enabled`, `timeout`, and `target`. Updates preserve the registration when its sequence, target, and platform stay the same.\n\n## Basic usage\n\n```vue\n<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { useHotkeySequence } from '@tanstack/vue-hotkeys'\n\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => {\n  window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'smooth' })\n})\n</script>\n```\n\n## Many sequences at once\n\nWhen you need several sequences, or a reactive list whose length changes, use `useHotkeySequences` instead of many `useHotkeySequence` calls. One composable registers every sequence safely.\n\n```vue\n<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { useHotkeySequences } from '@tanstack/vue-hotkeys'\n\nuseHotkeySequences([\n  { sequence: ['G', 'G'], callback: () => scrollToTop() },\n  { sequence: ['D', 'D'], callback: () => deleteLine(), options: { timeout: 500 } },\n])\n</script>\n```\n\nOptions merge like `useHotkeys`: `HotkeysProvider` defaults, then `commonOptions`, then each definition's `options`.\n\n## Matching steps\n\nBoth `SequenceManager` and `createSequenceMatcher` ignore modifier-only events, IME composition, and automatic keydown repeats. These events neither advance the sequence nor refresh its timeout: holding G does not complete a two-press G sequence. The manager prefers exact matches over weaker logical-key fallbacks while preserving equally strong matches.\n\n## Sequence options\n\n```ts\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], callback, {\n  timeout: 1000,\n  enabled: true,\n})\n```\n\n### Reactive `enabled`\n\nWhen disabled, the sequence stays registered (visible in devtools); only execution is suppressed.\n\n```vue\n<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { ref } from 'vue'\nimport { useHotkeySequence } from '@tanstack/vue-hotkeys'\n\nconst isVimMode = ref(true)\n\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop(), {\n  enabled: isVimMode,\n})\n</script>\n```\n\n## Global defaults via provider\n\n```vue\n<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { HotkeysProvider } from '@tanstack/vue-hotkeys'\n</script>\n\n<template>\n  <HotkeysProvider\n    :default-options=\"{\n      hotkeySequence: { timeout: 1500 },\n    }\"\n  >\n    <AppContent />\n  </HotkeysProvider>\n</template>\n```\n\n### `meta`\n\nSequences support the same `meta` option as hotkeys. Attach a `name` and `description` to use in shortcut palettes and devtools.\n\n```ts\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Go to Top', description: 'Scroll to the top of the page' },\n})\n```\n\nSee the [Hotkeys Guide](./hotkeys.md#metadata-name--description) for details on declaration merging and introspecting registrations.\n\n## Chained modifier chords\n\nThis example follows physical R and T positions. Brackets retain those positions even when the keys produce different letters. Other sequences can continue using logical characters.\n\nEach step can use modifiers (for example `Mod+K` then `Mod+C`). You can use the same modifier on consecutive steps:\n\n```ts\nuseHotkeySequence(['Shift+[KeyR]', 'Shift+[KeyT]'], () => doNextAction())\n```\n\nWhile a sequence is in progress, modifier-only keydown events (Shift, Control, Alt, or Meta pressed alone) are ignored: they do not advance the sequence and do not reset progress. A user can press Shift alone between `Shift+R` and `Shift+T` without breaking the sequence.\n\n## Common patterns\n\n### Vim-style navigation\n\n```ts\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop())\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'Shift+G'], () => scrollToBottom())\nuseHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine())\nuseHotkeySequence(['D', 'W'], () => deleteWord())\nuseHotkeySequence(['C', 'I', 'W'], () => changeInnerWord())\n```\n\n### Konami Code\n\n```ts\nuseHotkeySequence(\n  ['ArrowUp', 'ArrowUp', 'ArrowDown', 'ArrowDown', 'B', 'A'],\n  () => enableEasterEgg(),\n  { timeout: 2000 },\n)\n```\n\n## Under the hood\n\n`useHotkeySequence` uses the singleton `SequenceManager`. You can also access it directly:\n\n```ts\nimport { createSequenceMatcher, getSequenceManager } from '@tanstack/vue-hotkeys'\n\nconst manager = getSequenceManager()\nconst matcher = createSequenceMatcher(['G', 'G'], { timeout: 1000 })\n```\n\n### Conflicting registrations\n\nFor the same target, duplicate detection compares resolved steps: modifier aliases, modifier order, and logical key casing do not create separate bindings. `conflictBehavior` applies to equivalent sequences without rewriting their stored strings. Physical and logical identities remain distinct, and a shared prefix alone is not a duplicate registration. Recorder conflict detection also checks prefixes and observed physical/logical overlap.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Sequences Guide\nid: sequences\n---\n\nTanStack Hotkeys supports multi-key sequences in Vue, where keys are pressed one after another rather than simultaneously.\n\nSequence steps use the same string syntax as single hotkeys. For example, `['[KeyG]', '[KeyG]']` follows a physical position, while `['G', 'G']` follows the logical letter. A sequence can mix forms, such as `['Mod+[KeyK]', 'C']`. Display steps with `sequence.map((step) => formatForDisplay(step)).join(' → ')`.\n\n## Reactive options\n\nSequence options support the same [property getter patterns](./hotkeys.md#property-getters) as hotkey options, including `enabled`, `timeout`, and `target`. Updates preserve the registration when its sequence, target, and platform stay the same.\n\n## Basic usage\n\n```vue\n<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { useHotkeySequence } from '@tanstack/vue-hotkeys'\n\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => {\n  window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'smooth' })\n})\n</script>\n```\n\n## Many sequences at once\n\nWhen you need several sequences, or a reactive list whose length changes, use `useHotkeySequences` instead of many `useHotkeySequence` calls. One composable registers every sequence safely.\n\n```vue\n<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { useHotkeySequences } from '@tanstack/vue-hotkeys'\n\nuseHotkeySequences([\n  { sequence: ['G', 'G'], callback: () => scrollToTop() },\n  { sequence: ['D', 'D'], callback: () => deleteLine(), options: { timeout: 500 } },\n])\n</script>\n```\n\nOptions merge like `useHotkeys`: `HotkeysProvider` defaults, then `commonOptions`, then each definition's `options`.\n\n## Matching steps\n\nBoth `SequenceManager` and `createSequenceMatcher` ignore modifier-only events, IME composition, and automatic keydown repeats. These events neither advance the sequence nor refresh its timeout: holding G does not complete a two-press G sequence. The manager prefers exact matches over weaker logical-key fallbacks while preserving equally strong matches.\n\n## Sequence options\n\n```ts\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], callback, {\n  timeout: 1000,\n  enabled: true,\n})\n```\n\n### Reactive `enabled`\n\nWhen disabled, the sequence stays registered (visible in devtools); only execution is suppressed.\n\n```vue\n<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { ref } from 'vue'\nimport { useHotkeySequence } from '@tanstack/vue-hotkeys'\n\nconst isVimMode = ref(true)\n\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop(), {\n  enabled: isVimMode,\n})\n</script>\n```\n\n## Global defaults via provider\n\n```vue\n<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { HotkeysProvider } from '@tanstack/vue-hotkeys'\n</script>\n\n<template>\n  <HotkeysProvider\n    :default-options=\"{\n      hotkeySequence: { timeout: 1500 },\n    }\"\n  >\n    <AppContent />\n  </HotkeysProvider>\n</template>\n```\n\n### `meta`\n\nSequences support the same `meta` option as hotkeys. Attach a `name` and `description` to use in shortcut palettes and devtools.\n\n```ts\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Go to Top', description: 'Scroll to the top of the page' },\n})\n```\n\nSee the [Hotkeys Guide](./hotkeys.md#metadata-name--description) for details on declaration merging and introspecting registrations.\n\n## Chained modifier chords\n\nThis example follows physical R and T positions. Brackets retain those positions even when the keys produce different letters. Other sequences can continue using logical characters.\n\nEach step can use modifiers (for example `Mod+K` then `Mod+C`). You can use the same modifier on consecutive steps:\n\n```ts\nuseHotkeySequence(['Shift+[KeyR]', 'Shift+[KeyT]'], () => doNextAction())\n```\n\nWhile a sequence is in progress, modifier-only keydown events (Shift, Control, Alt, or Meta pressed alone) are ignored: they do not advance the sequence and do not reset progress. A user can press Shift alone between `Shift+R` and `Shift+T` without breaking the sequence.\n\n## Common patterns\n\n### Vim-style navigation\n\n```ts\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop())\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'Shift+G'], () => scrollToBottom())\nuseHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine())\nuseHotkeySequence(['D', 'W'], () => deleteWord())\nuseHotkeySequence(['C', 'I', 'W'], () => changeInnerWord())\n```\n\n### Konami Code\n\n```ts\nuseHotkeySequence(\n  ['ArrowUp', 'ArrowUp', 'ArrowDown', 'ArrowDown', 'B', 'A'],\n  () => enableEasterEgg(),\n  { timeout: 2000 },\n)\n```\n\n## Under the hood\n\n`useHotkeySequence` uses the singleton `SequenceManager`. You can also access it directly:\n\n```ts\nimport { createSequenceMatcher, getSequenceManager } from '@tanstack/vue-hotkeys'\n\nconst manager = getSequenceManager()\nconst matcher = createSequenceMatcher(['G', 'G'], { timeout: 1000 })\n```\n\n### Conflicting registrations\n\nFor the same target, duplicate detection compares resolved steps: modifier aliases, modifier order, and logical key casing do not create separate bindings. `conflictBehavior` applies to equivalent sequences without rewriting their stored strings. Physical and logical identities remain distinct, and a shared prefix alone is not a duplicate registration. Recorder conflict detection also checks prefixes and observed physical/logical overlap.\n"}
